\description{
Computes overlap counts between two to five peak sets for generating Venn
diagrams. This function is used internally by \code{\link{makeVennDiagram}} and
\code{\link{findOverlapsOfPeaks}} but can also be called directly to obtain counts
without visualization.
}
\details{
The function identifies overlapping peaks across multiple sets and counts how many
peaks fall into each possible overlap category (e.g., peaks unique to set 1, peaks
shared by sets 1 and 2, peaks shared by all sets, etc.). The counting method depends
on the \code{connectedPeaks} parameter, which handles cases where multiple peaks
from different sets form connected/overlapping groups.

\strong{Overlap calculation methods:}

Overlaps can be calculated at three levels, specified by the \code{by} parameter:
\itemize{
  \item \code{"region"} (default): Based on genomic coordinates (chromosome,
        start, end). Uses \code{findOverlaps()} to identify overlapping ranges.
        This is the most common approach for ChIP-seq peak analysis. For large
        datasets (>10,000 peaks), the function processes peaks chromosome by
        chromosome for efficiency.
  \item \code{"feature"}: Based on feature annotations stored in the \code{feature}
        metadata column of the GRanges object. Peaks are considered overlapping
        if they share the same feature identifier (e.g., same gene ID). Useful
        when peaks are annotated with gene IDs or other feature identifiers and
        you want to find overlaps based on shared features rather than genomic
        coordinates. Note: This requires a \code{feature} column in the metadata.
  \item \code{"base"}: At nucleotide-level resolution, using \code{disjoin()} to
        create non-overlapping intervals and calculating the actual number of
        overlapping base pairs. The counts represent the total number of base pairs
        in each overlap category, weighted by the width of overlapping regions.
        More computationally intensive but provides precise overlap measurements.
}



\strong{Input handling:}
\itemize{
  \item Peak sets can be provided as separate GRanges arguments or as a single list
  \item Peak set names are automatically generated if missing
  \item Duplicate peak set names are not allowed
  \item The function validates that 2-5 peak sets are provided
}

\strong{Algorithm:}
\itemize{
  \item For \code{by = "region"}: Uses graph-based connected component analysis
        to identify groups of overlapping peaks across sets. For large datasets,
        processes peaks chromosome by chromosome for efficiency.
  \item For \code{by = "feature"}: Groups peaks by shared feature identifiers
        from the metadata column.
  \item For \code{by = "base"}: Uses \code{disjoin()} to create non-overlapping
        intervals and calculates weighted counts based on interval widths.
}
}
